Different Perspectives on How to Tackle Environmental Issues
Shallow-green Ecology vs Deep-green Ecology
Shallow-green Ecology
Shallow-green ecology, sometimes referred to as “environmental pragmatism” or “light green”
environmentalism, advocates for addressing environmental issues within the framework of existing
social and economic systems. It is grounded in the belief that environmental protection can and
should coexist with economic growth and development. Shallow-green ecology promotes practical,
often market-based solutions to reduce environmental impacts, such as emissions trading
schemes, pollution controls, and investment in cleaner technologies. The focus is on sustainable
development, aiming to mitigate the environmental impacts of human activities without
fundamentally changing industrialised society or the global economic system.
https://www.politicsexplained.co.uk/ 3
● One of the most prominent examples of shallow-green ecology in practice is the Paris
Agreement, which allows countries to set their own emissions reduction targets (Nationally
Determined Contributions, or NDCs) and encourages investment in green technology and
renewable energy as paths to sustainable growth.
● Many developed nations, as well as multinational corporations, embrace this perspective,
seeing climate action as compatible with economic objectives.
○ For example, the European Union’s Emissions Trading System (ETS), which caps
overall emissions while allowing companies to buy and sell emissions allowances,
exemplifies a shallow-green approach that leverages market mechanisms to
incentivise emissions reduction within a capitalist framework.
While shallow-green ecology has led to significant advancements, particularly in terms of
technological innovation, it is often criticised by deep-green advocates for not addressing the root
causes of environmental degradation and not being radical enough to prevent the destruction of
the planet. Nonetheless, this approach is widely shared by important global actors such as the EU,
the United States, and large corporations, who view environmental reform as a gradual process
that can align with their economic interests.
Deep-green Ecology
Deep-green ecology, also known as “radical environmentalism” or “dark green” environmentalism,
advocates for a more transformative approach to environmental issues. Rooted in the belief that
the current economic and industrial systems are inherently unsustainable, deep-green ecology
calls for a fundamental restructuring of society’s relationship with nature. This perspective argues
that shallow-green ecology is insufficient, as it prioritises human needs and economic growth over
the health of ecosystems.
● Deep-green ecologists promote policies and lifestyle changes that minimise human impact
on the planet, often advocating for significant reductions in consumption, shifts away from
fossil fuels, and a move towards local, self-sufficient communities.
○ This perspective is associated with the biocentric view, which places equal intrinsic
value on all living things and ecosystems.
○ Deep-green ecology also tends to reject market-based solutions, arguing that they
perpetuate the very systems of exploitation that lead to environmental degradation.
● Examples of deep-green thinking can be seen in the degrowth movement, which advocates
for downsizing economies to reduce environmental impact and promote a simpler, more
ecologically balanced lifestyle.
○ Influential figures like Arne Næss, the Norwegian philosopher who coined the term
“deep ecology,” have argued for this transformative approach, stating that true
ecological sustainability requires a fundamental shift in values, from prioritising
human needs to respecting the interconnectedness of all life.
While deep-green ecology has inspired environmental activism and grassroots movements, it
remains a minority view among major global actors. Few governments or corporations fully
embrace this perspective, as it often challenges economic growth and their interests. However, the
perspective has gained traction among youth climate activists and some environmental
organisations, who argue only radical changes can avert catastrophic environmental outcomes.
