Tragedy Of The Commons vs Sustainable Development
Tragedy of the Commons
The Tragedy of the Commons is a theory that explains why the international community struggles
to share resources sustainably and suggests that the states won’t effectively come together to
tackle climate change as it is against their interests. Popularised by ecologist Garrett Hardin in
1968, the tragedy of the commons describes how, when individuals act in their self-interest to
maximise resource use, they often deplete or degrade the shared resources upon which they all
depend. In the context of environmental issues, this theory suggests that since resources like air,
oceans, and biodiversity are collectively owned, they are vulnerable to overuse because individual
actors—whether countries, corporations, or individuals—lack sufficient incentives to limit their
exploitation for the common good.
● This concept explains the difficulties in global climate action, where countries may prioritise
short-term economic gains over long-term environmental sustainability.
○ For example, fossil fuel-dependent countries like Saudi Arabia and Russia have
historically resisted international efforts to reduce carbon emissions due to the
economic benefits of oil and gas exports.
● Similarly, many industrialised nations have continued high emissions, recognising that
transitioning to cleaner energy may involve substantial upfront costs and economic
restructuring.
● The reluctance of individual nations to reduce emissions without assurance that others will
follow illustrates the core of the tragedy of the commons: the tendency for shared resources
to be overused and neglected when responsibility is diffuse.
The past two decades have demonstrated how the tragedy of the commons plays out in global
climate efforts. Despite international agreements like the Kyoto Protocol and the Paris Agreement,
global greenhouse gas emissions continue to rise, with many countries failing to meet their targets.
This pattern reflects a central issue highlighted by the theory: without strong mechanisms for
accountability and incentives for collective action, countries may continue to prioritise national
interests over global environmental needs, contributing to the persistent challenge of addressing
climate change effectively.
Sustainable Development
Sustainable development is an approach to environmental management that aims to reconcile
economic growth with environmental protection, arguing that economic development and tackling
climate change can sustainibly coexist. This concept was popularised by the Brundtland Report in
https://www.politicsexplained.co.uk/ 5
1987, which defined sustainable development as “development that meets the needs of the
present without compromising the ability of future generations to meet their own needs.” Unlike the
tragedy of the commons theory, which emphasises the inherent conflict between individual gain
and collective wellbeing, sustainable development is rooted in the belief that protecting the
environment and economic prosperity can reinforce each other.
● The sustainable development approach has shaped international efforts to address climate
change and has been central to global agreements like the Paris Agreement and the United
Nations Sustainable Development Goals (SDGs).
○ For example, SDG 13 focuses specifically on climate action, aiming to integrate
climate measures into national policies and strengthen resilience to climate-related
hazards.
● Sustainable development advocates argue that by investing in green technology, renewable
energy, and sustainable agriculture, nations can achieve economic growth while reducing
their environmental footprint. The rise of renewable energy sources such as solar and wind,
which accounted for 29% of global electricity generation in 2020, exemplifies the potential
of this approach to deliver both economic and environmental benefits.
○ Countries like Denmark and Costa Rica have shown that ambitious national policies
centred on sustainability can lead to substantial progress.
○ Costa Rica regularly generates over 98% of its electricity from renewable sources
and has used its success to promote eco-tourism, enhancing its economy while
preserving its natural resources.
However, critics argue that sustainable development does not fully address the scale of change
required to tackle the climate crisis, as it assumes that economic growth can continue indefinitely
without harming the environment. Deep-green ecologists argue that the current model of
sustainable development is still too reliant on resource extraction and consumerism, and that only
a reduction in consumption and a restructuring of economic systems can truly address
environmental degradation. So far, a sustainable development approach has failed to prove that it
can be effective and overcome the tragedy of the commons, as the world is still on a path to
environmental destruction, with global temperatures expected to rise 2.7°C by the end of the
century.
Sovereignty As An Obstacle To International Co-operation And
Tackling Climate Chnage
● The importance of sovereignty within the international system means that international
climate agreements have had to rely on voluntary commitments by states rather than being
able to create legally binding commitments.
○ For example, the Paris Climate Agreement allows each country to set its own
Nationally Determined Contributions (NDCs) and has no enforceable penalties for
countries that fall short of their targets.
● This reliance on voluntary commitments has been a significant obstacle to effective climate
action, as it allows nations to scale back or delay their goals based on domestic priorities or
economic pressures without facing international consequences.
● Countries with high emissions, such as China, India, and the United States, have also been
able to set targets that may fall short of what scientists recommend to limit global warming,
undermining the collective goal of keeping temperature increases within 1.5°C.
https://www.politicsexplained.co.uk/ 6
Case Study - United States
The United States’ climate policy has reflected its prioritisation of national sovereignty across
various international agreements. In 1997, the U.S. signed the Kyoto Protocol but ultimately
refused to ratify it, with the Senate arguing that the Protocol unfairly exempted developing
countries from emissions reductions and could harm the U.S. economy. This decision set a
precedent for prioritising domestic economic concerns over international climate obligations.
Under the Paris Agreement, the U.S. initially committed to reducing emissions by 26-28% below
2005 levels by 2025, but this commitment was rescinded when President Donald Trump withdrew
from the Agreement in 2017. This withdrawal highlighted how sovereignty enables countries to
shift climate commitments according to their domestic political climate, undermining consistency
in agreeing to and meeting international agreements. When President Joe Biden rejoined the
Paris Agreement in 2021 with a renewed target of reducing emissions by 50-52% by 2030, the
lack of a binding enforcement mechanism meant this target remained subject to potential future
political changes and it is likely to be rescinded after Trump’s reelection, as Trump has promised
to increase production of fossil fuels.
Case Study - Japan
Japan’s engagement with international climate agreements has been shaped by its sovereign
prioritisation of energy security, especially following the 2011 Fukushima nuclear disaster.
Although Japan initially committed to emissions reductions under the Kyoto Protocol, it struggled
to meet its targets after Fukushima forced a shift from nuclear to fossil fuels. This led Japan to
increase its use of coal and natural gas, which conflicted with its climate commitments. In 2015,
Japan joined the Paris Agreement and set an emissions reduction target of 46% from 2013 levels
by 2030, but it has continued investing in coal-fired power plants domestically and abroad, citing
sovereignty over its energy policy and the need to prioritise its economic interests.
Disagreement Over Who Should Accept The Responsibility
And Pay As An Obstacle To International Co-operation and
Tackling Climate Change
The Views of the Developing World
Developing nations have long argued that they are disproportionately affected by climate change
despite having contributed minimally to the problem. They contend that developed countries, which
emitted huge amounts of CO₂ to develop their economies, should bear the primary responsibility
for both reducing emissions and providing financial support to help less affluent countries adapt to
https://www.politicsexplained.co.uk/ 7
the impacts of climate change. This position is grounded in principles of fairness and equity, with
developing nations advocating for a framework that recognises their limited contributions to
historical emissions and their constrained resources to combat climate change effectively.
Who Developing Countries Believe Is Responsible and Should Pay
● Developing countries argue that developed nations are primarily responsible for the current
state of the global climate crisis due to their high levels of greenhouse gas emissions during
the Industrial Revolution and beyond.
● This perspective has formed the basis for the Common but Differentiated Responsibilities
(CBDR) principle, enshrined in international climate agreements such as the United Nations
Framework Convention on Climate Change (UNFCCC) and the Kyoto Protocol.
○ According to the CBDR principle, while all countries share the obligation to address
climate change, developed countries should take the lead because of their historical
emissions and greater financial resources.
● For example, between 1850 and 2019, the United States and European Union countries
together accounted for approximately 50% of global cumulative CO₂ emissions, while Africa
contributed less than 4% and South Asia only 3%.
○ For developing nations, this disparity underscores the belief that developed
countries have benefitted from decades of unrestrained industrialisation at the
expense of the environment and thus should bear a larger share of the financial
burden for climate action.
● The Green Climate Fund (GCF), established under the UNFCCC, was created with this
purpose in mind, aiming to mobilise $100 billion annually by 2020 to assist developing
nations. Developed countries failed to meet this goal on time, reaching only around $83.3
billion in 2020 and only meeting this target by 2022.
How Developing States Believe They Should Have A Right to Grow Economically
Developing nations maintain that, despite their participation in climate action, they should not be
expected to compromise their economic growth, which is essential to lifting millions out of poverty
and meeting basic human needs.
● Many of these countries are still in the process of industrialising, relying on carbon-intensive
industries to develop infrastructure, support economic stability, and create jobs.
○ This view is often presented in terms of climate justice, which argues that it is
inequitable to ask developing countries to limit growth when they bear little historical
responsibility for the climate crisis; Africa, for example, has contributed less than 4%
to global cumulative CO₂ emissions.
● For instance, Sub-Saharan Africa faces an energy access gap, with nearly 600 million
people still lacking reliable electricity, making industrialisation and economic stability a
pressing priority.
● Leaders from countries like India, which has a GDP per capita of roughly $2,000 compared
to the $65,000 per capita in the United States, and Nigeria, where around 40% of the
population lives below the poverty line, argue that while they are committed to reducing
emissions, they must be allowed more time and flexibility to reach these goals in a way that
does not hinder economic development.
○ For example, India’s Nationally Determined Contribution (NDC) under the Paris
Agreement allows it to increase emissions until it reaches its peak by 2030,
balancing its developmental needs with its climate commitments.
https://www.politicsexplained.co.uk/ 8
● Developing nations therefore advocate for an approach that allows them to grow while
gradually transitioning to cleaner energy sources, supported by substantial financial and
technological assistance from developed nations.
The Views of the Developed World
Developed nations, historically the largest contributors to greenhouse gas emissions, recognise
their role in climate change but argue that addressing the crisis should be a shared global
responsibility. Developed countries generally advocate for a balanced approach, where both
developed and developing nations contribute to emissions reductions. This position is based on the
view that while developed nations should take the lead, emerging economies with increasing
emissions, like China and India, must also commit to ambitious climate goals to prevent further
global warming. It is of course also not in their economic interests to bear the total burden of
tackling climate change.
Who Developed Countries Believe Is Responsible and Should Pay
● While developed nations acknowledge that they have contributed disproportionately to
historical emissions, they often argue that the climate crisis now requires collective
responsibility, with all nations committing to significant emissions reductions.
○ This stance reflects a shift in focus from historical emissions to current and
projected emissions growth, especially in rapidly industrialising countries.
● They argue that while they should play a leading role in providing climate finance,
developing countries with growing emissions should also bear some responsibility for
climate action, given their increasing contributions to global greenhouse gas emissions.
○ For instance, as of recent data, China accounts for approximately 30% of current
global CO₂ emissions, while India contributes around 7%, surpassing the combined
emissions of the entire European Union, which produces about 8%.
○ This viewpoint was prominent in the Copenhagen Accord (2009), where developed
nations advocated for voluntary commitments from all countries, including major
emerging economies, to address the growing contributions from countries with high
emissions growth.
● In line with this view, developed countries have provided some climate finance through
mechanisms like the Green Climate Fund (GCF), but often argue that their contributions
must be matched by meaningful commitments from developing countries.
Has the Debate Between the Developing and Developed World Been an
Obstacle to International Cooperation and Agreement?
The longstanding debate between developed and developing nations over responsibility and
financial obligations has been one of the most significant challenges in reaching comprehensive
and effective climate agreements. While both groups recognise the urgent need for climate action,
their differing perspectives on responsibility, finance, and equity have often resulted in contentious
negotiations. This section explores how this debate has influenced international climate
agreements and assesses its role as an obstacle to cooperation.
Early Obstacles in the Kyoto Protocol:
The Kyoto Protocol (1997) was one of the earliest major international agreements to address
climate change. Under the protocol, only developed countries were required to reduce their
emissions, reflecting the principle of Common but Differentiated Responsibilities (CBDR).
https://www.politicsexplained.co.uk/ 9
Developing countries successfully argued that their low historical emissions and economic needs
should exempt them from binding targets.
However, this differentiation created significant division. The United States, at the time one of the
largest greenhouse gas emitters, ultimately refused to ratify the Kyoto Protocol, citing concerns
over economic competitiveness and the absence of binding commitments for rapidly industrialising
nations like China and India. The limited participation and lack of legally binding commitments for
developing nations highlighted early tensions between the two groups, and these disagreements
set a precedent that would continue to shape climate negotiations. The reluctance of some
developed countries to commit without comparable obligations from emerging economies
demonstrated how differing views on responsibility could hinder broad-based participation and
enforcement in climate agreements.
The Shift in the Copenhagen Accord:
The Copenhagen Accord (2009) represented a shift in climate diplomacy as developed countries
pushed for voluntary commitments from all nations, not just developed ones. The Copenhagen
Accord did not establish binding emissions targets but encouraged both developed and developing
countries to set voluntary emissions reduction goals. Developed nations argued that rapidly
growing economies, especially China and India, should commit to climate action to prevent future
emissions increases from undermining global progress.
Although the accord included pledges of financial support for developing countries, it was criticised
for lacking enforcement mechanisms and clear accountability measures. Many developing nations
felt that the accord failed to address their historical arguments about responsibility and financial
support. The accord’s voluntary nature and failure to achieve unanimous support highlighted the
challenges of bridging the gap between developed and developing countries, as mutual distrust
persisted over fairness and accountability.
The Paris Agreement: Progress with Persistent Tensions:
The Paris Agreement (2015) marked a significant milestone in climate cooperation, as it
established a universal framework that required all countries to set Nationally Determined
Contributions (NDCs). The agreement retained the CBDR principle, allowing flexibility in emissions
targets and enabling countries to commit based on national circumstances. For example, India’s
Nationally Determined Contribution (NDC) under the Paris Agreement allows it to increase
emissions until it reaches its peak by 2030, balancing its developmental needs with its climate
commitments.
Developing countries viewed the Paris Agreement as an improvement, as it included provisions for
climate finance, with developed nations pledging to mobilise $100 billion annually by 2020.
However, when this target was not met until 2022, tensions rose again, with developing nations
expressing frustration over unfulfilled promises and developed nations arguing that if they are
providing funding, then developing nations must set ambitious climate goals. Mia Mottley, the
Prime Minister of Barbados, argued that the $100 billion target is insufficient and that financial
support from developed states should be increased to reflect the scale of the climate crisis.
Developed countries also saw the Paris Agreement as an improvement as it required all countries
to submit Nationally Determined Contributions (NDCs) and commit to regular progress reviews.
The EU pushed for ambitious commitments from other countries, similar to their own legally
binding emissions reduction targets of 55% below 1990 levels by 2030.
https://www.politicsexplained.co.uk/ 10
Despite the Paris Agreement’s achievements, its voluntary structure and non-binding targets mean
that disparities in climate commitments remain, with some high-emitting countries setting less
ambitious targets. This has raised concerns among developing countries, who argue that without
stronger accountability mechanisms, developed nations could fall short of their obligations without
repercussions, undermining global progress toward the 1.5°C goal.
The Call for Loss and Damage Compensation and the COP27 Agreement:
One of the most divisive issues in recent years has been the call for loss and damage
compensation. Developing nations, especially vulnerable countries within the Climate Vulnerable
Forum (CVF) and Alliance of Small Island States (AOSIS), have long advocated for a dedicated
fund to compensate them for the irreversible impacts of climate change. At COP26 in Glasgow, this
demand gained international attention, but developed countries, particularly the United States and
the European Union, resisted it, arguing that such a fund could imply legal liability for historical
emissions. The resistance to a formal loss and damage mechanism reflects developed nations’
reluctance to take on additional financial burdens or potential liabilities
After sustained advocacy, the Loss and Damage Fund was finally established at COP27 in 2022,
representing a breakthrough for developing nations. However, significant questions remain over
how this fund will be financed and managed, as developed countries have yet to commit concrete
funding sources. This unresolved debate on compensation underscores how differing perspectives
on historical responsibility continue to complicate cooperation, even in the face of a globally
recognised climate crisis.
Overall Assessment:
Overall, it is clear that the division between developing states and developed states over the
responsibility for climate change has slowed down effective measures to tackle climate change.
Every conference/agreement involves significant tensions and the need for compromises that have
https://www.politicsexplained.co.uk/ 11
stood in the way of the international community having an agreed strategy and pursuing legally
binding commitments on all countries.
This being said, there has been substantial progress despite this division, particularly with the Paris
Climate Agreement and Loss and Damage Fund. Compromise has taken place, with developed
states recognising they will need to financially support developing states which should be allowed
to set less ambitious targets and developing states accepting that all states must accept some of
the burden for tackling climate change, rather than just developed states.
